message.content:
I found two likely periapical lesions, both in the lower jaw. I identified them by eye on cropped, contrast-enhanced views of the image, so treat this as a screening read, not a diagnosis.

1. **Lower left first molar (patient's left, right side of the image), PAI 4:** a fairly well-defined dark (radiolucent) area surrounds the root tips of both roots. It sits just past the gap where a tooth is missing.
2. **Lower right first molar (patient's right, left side of the image), PAI 3:** a less sharply bordered dark area runs from between the roots down to the tip of the front (mesial) root. This one could instead be bone loss between the roots from gum disease, or a combined tooth-and-gum lesion. That makes both the call and the grade less certain.

I did not count the dark area between the roots of the lower right second molar, because it looks like gum-disease bone loss rather than a lesion at the root tip. The upper teeth sit behind crowns and overlapping structures, and I saw no clear lesions there.

